  "ai_language_tip": "Start in English in Kenya; Swahili (sw) in Tanzania and with many Kenyan small businesses; Amharic (am) in Addis Ababa (English in hotels and larger firms); French in Madagascar (Malagasy for small businesses) and in Haiti for formal businesses, Haitian Creole (ht) for most everyday calls.",
  "english_ok": "sometimes — Good in Kenya and in hotels/tour firms in Tanzania and Ethiopia; rare in Madagascar and Haiti, where French or the local language is expected.",
  "politeness": "Greetings come first and can be long (ke/tz: 'Habari'/'Shikamoo' to elders; et: 'Selam' with polite 'እርስዎ'; mg: 'Manao ahoana'; ht: 'Bonjou'/'Bonswa'); use 'Sir/Madam', 'Monsieur/Madame'; close with thanks.",
  "name_tip": "Bookings often under first name; many Ethiopians have no family surname (given name + father's name), and Malagasy names can be very long — spell slowly or send by WhatsApp/SMS.",
  "phone_tip": "ke 07XX XXX XXX or 01XX XXX XXX (+254); tz 07XX XXX XXX or 06XX (+255); et 09XX XX XX XX (+251); mg 03X XX XXX XX (+261); ht 8 digits XXXX XXXX (+509); WhatsApp and SMS are the usual confirmation channels.",
  "time_tip": "CRITICAL: Swahili time (tz, ke) and Ethiopian time (et) count from about 6:00 am — 'saa mbili asubuhi' = 8:00 and Ethiopian '2 ሰዓት' in the morning = 8:00; always confirm in 24h international time. Ethiopia also uses its own calendar (about 7–8 years behind, 13 months).",
  "number_tip": "Numbers usually given in English or French digits; M-Pesa and mobile-money codes are long — never read them aloud, send by message; in Malagasy numbers are traditionally said from units upward.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid calls after 20:00, Friday midday prayer in Muslim-majority areas (Zanzibar, coast), Sunday mornings (church) in ke/et/mg/ht, and Ramadan iftar hour on the Swahili coast.",
  "closed_days": "Weekend Saturday–Sunday in all five, many shops open Saturday and some Sunday; holidays differ widely (e.g. ke 1 June and 20 October, tz 7 July/Saba Saba, et Ethiopian Christmas 7 January and Enkutatash in September, mg 26 June, ht 1 January and Carnival).",
  "booking_channels": "Phone, WhatsApp and walk-in dominate; M-Pesa (ke, tz) and Telebirr (et) for deposits; Glovo, Uber Eats or Bolt Food in Nairobi and some other cities; Uber and Bolt in Nairobi, Dar es Salaam; few online booking platforms in mg and ht.",
  "voice_menu": "Telecoms (Safaricom, Airtel, Vodacom, Ethio telecom, Telma, Digicel, Natcom), banks and utilities use IVR; small businesses answer directly on mobiles.",
  "callback_habits": "Confirmations are by WhatsApp or SMS, often with a mobile-money payment request; hotels and tour operators e-mail vouchers.",
